rst 7 latent frames? If the sampler draws noise for the whole latent with a seeded RNG, the first-22-frame slice of the 107-frame noise should match the 22-frame run's noise IF the RNG fills row-major. Then after 1 step, compare the 107-run's first-chunk latents to the 22-run's latents: if they diverge immediately, the corruption enters inside the DiT/sampler, not VAE. And if the divergence is large even at step 1 for early frames, that's positional/global mixing, not VAE chunking.\n\n**H2. A length-dependent indexing/stride bug in the C port** (flattening order [T,H,W] vs [H,W,T], patchify reshape, RoPE phase index, attention mask) that happens to be benign for small T because of a coincidence (e.g., special-cased path for T≤12 or dimensions that coincide: note at T=7 vs 12; maybe the bug appears when T×something exceeds a tile/loop bound). But careful: would an indexing bug produce *flat brown mush* rather than garbage? A hard indexing bug usually gives structured garbage or repeated tiles, not smooth mush. But \"faint tile seams\" suggests something structured. Yet 124 gives dark noise — different regime. A subtle RoPE phase offset bug (positions computed mod something, or using pixel-frame index instead of latent index) would produce mush that worsens with T. Hmm — e.g., temporal RoPE applied with position = latent index × (some scale) — if the port computes positions assuming 4× compression but actual is 17/5 non-uniform... but then 22 and 39 would be wrong too. Unless the position mapping is right for T≤12 by coincidence? Eh.\n\nCheaper discriminating check for H1 vs H2: **compare against reference outputs at matched short length, then at long length with the reference's own long-video path**. If the port matches reference bit-near at 22/39 but the reference never runs full-length DiT (uses windows), H1 confirmed structurally without any video generation.\n\nAlso: does upstream antirez/h3.c even support >22 frames? The user patched it for eGPU; maybe upstream 