m fine. Numerical blowup would give NaN/inf or channel outliers; dump latents and scan for NaN/inf and per-channel max. Low-contrast mush is *not* what raw numerical blowup gives — mush is more like over-smoothed attention (positional) or wrong guidance scale. But one numerical candidate: attention logits in BF16 with large RoPE-rotated values → range issues? They said MPSGraph BF16 matmul bit-exact and accumulations in float. Softmax range grows as sqrt(seq) — no. I'd rank pure numerical low. Distinguishing measurement: per-channel min/max/NaN count on dumped latents and on one attention block's output at T=12 vs T=32; numerical blowup shows outliers/NaN and logit scale growth; positional bug shows finite, smooth, wrong values with attention entropy collapse. Specifically: compute attention-row entropy or the cosine similarity between DiT output at T=7-window vs full-T for the same first chunk — if full-T output for chunk 0 deviates smoothly (no outliers), positional.\n\n**H5. Sampler/guidance/config bug dependent on T**: e.g., sigma schedule or guidance applied per-frame with a buffer sized by T, or the negative-prompt branch sliced wrong when T> some batching threshold. At 124 the encode blowup hints a *different code path* triggers between T=32 and 37 — e.g., an allocation that spills, MPSGraph falling back to CPU for some op, or the graph cache missing and recompiling per block (7.9 s CPU per block smells like **MPSGraph recompilation**: when a graph's shape changes, MPSGraph recompiles; if some buffer shape depends on T and crosses an internal threshold — or the code accidentally recreates the graph each block when T large — compile happens on the encode thread). 1582 s/200 = 7.9 s per block of pure CPU inside encode — graph specialization/compile is the classic cause. Why would T=37 trigger recompiles but not 32? E.g., attention kernel selection changes at a size threshold (tiled vs untiled), or a padding rule changes shape class (e.g., next-multiple-of-X padding: 32→ pad bucket changes at >32!). **T=32 is a power of two; T=37 is not.** If any internal padding/alignment buckets T to powers of two or multiples of 32, then T=37 changes bucket → new shapes → recompile per encode → 7.9 s CPU/block, GPU 24% busy. And the weight stream collapse is a *symptom* of the GPU being starved (encode thread busy compiling), consistent with 0.090 GiB/s measured while moving same bytes. So H6: the 124 anomaly is a shape-bucketing/recompilation artifact triggered when latent_t crosses 32 — independent of corruption (107 corrupt without it). This explains \"both\" only if the corruption mechanism also has a threshold at ≤32... but 107 (T=32) is corrupt and 39 (T=12) clean; if corruption onset is also at a 16-or-32 boundary, then one mechanism: something switches path at T>16 or T≥32. But we don't know 56/73/90 yet — onset could be at T=17, 22, or 27. If onset at T=17 (56 frames) that's *not* a power-of-two bucket; if at T=27, also not. Bisection would locate it.\n\nHmm wait — could the corruption onset and the encode blowup share a root at the **32-latent boundary**, with corruption onset actually at T=32 exactly (i.e., 56/73/90 all clean)? Then one mechanism: crossing T>31 flips some code path (kernel selection, tiling, bucketing) that (a) computes wrong results and (b) at T=37 also changes shapes causing recompiles. But why would the path at T=32 (107f) be corrupt *without* recompiles? If the wrong path is shape-specialized and cached after first compile, encode stays fast; at 37 the shape changes per block? No — shape is fixed per run. Recompile per *block visit* (200 visits) with same shapes means the graph cache is being invalidated every block, or graphs are recreated per block when some condition holds (e.g., total graph count exceeds a cache size; or memory pressure causes MPSGraph to discard compiled kernels; or the heuristic \"specialize on shapes\" gets disabled when... ). Alternatively the CPU time is not compile but **allocation churn**: at T=37 some buffer can't fit in VRAM (16 GiB; VAE peak 9.365, DiT peak 2.289 — but MPSGraph may allocate big intermediates) and falls back to *pageable/host memory*, causing the encode thread to block on mapping/zeroing, and GPU starves. Metal on macOS: when a device runs out of VRAM, allocations still succeed (managed via residency), performance craters; eGPU over TB3 makes host-mapped memory especially slow. The weight stream descheduling (0.09 GiB/s) fits GPU idle waiting on CPU encode. But why per-block 7.9 s consistently? A big host-side memset/copy per block could do it.\n\nBut the user explicitly notes: this anomaly absent at 107 which is corrupt → encode blowup cannot be the cause. They ask whether one mechanism explains both. Best answer: treat as independent unless a T≥33 threshold exists for both; the corruption exists at T=32 without the encode anomaly, so a single mechanism would need a *second* threshold at ~33-37. The simplest single-mechanism story (positional OOD for corruption + shape-bucket recompile at T=37 for encode) is really two mechanisms. Occam: independent. But note the encode blowup *itself* is worth fixing via checking MPSGraph graph cache hits per block and `MTLDevice` residency — and it's diagnosable cheaply: log graph-cache hit/miss per block, or sample the main thread during the 124 encode (spindump/Instruments) to see if time is in MPSGraph compile (`MPSGraphExecutable` specialize) vs `MTLBuffer` allocation vs `IOSurface`/memcpy. Cheapest: `sample` the process for 30 s during the slow phase — one stack tells you compile vs alloc vs memcpy. That's decisive for the encode anomaly.\n\nNow the user's worry in \"Relevant implementation detail\": commitAndContinue retiring roots — could a buffer's contents not persist across a commit boundary? Metal guarantees buffer contents persist across command buffer commits *within the same device/queue* — commits are ordered on the same queue; writes complete before subsequent command buffers read (same queue serialization). So no, commit boundaries don't corrupt buffer contents on the same queue. Caveat: if the code uses the *new* root's completion before waiting, or reuses an MPSGraph *temporary* tensor across encode calls (MPSGraph may alias/recycle temp buffers only within a graph execution; a tensor handed out across commits without retaining could be recycled) — that would be a real bug but would break at 22 frames too (400 roots already at 22). Not length-dependent. So commitAndContinue is almost certainly innocent; state plainly: Metal serializes same-queue command buffers; persistence across commits is guaranteed for MTLBuffer storage. The thing to check instead is whether any *activation* buffer is reused with `MTLResourceStorageModeShared` + overwritten by CPU while GPU reads (race) — but again not T-dependent.\n\nNow: what's actually special about T=12 → 32? Let me think about \"trained 22-frame decoder chunk\" again: error message at h3.c:861 says generation requires at least one trained 22-frame *decoder* chunk — that's about the VAE decoder being trained with 22-frame windows? And `h3_align_frame_count` snaps to 5+17k. In upstream antirez/h3.c, I believe the design decodes video in overlapping 22-frame chunks (17 new + 5 overlap? or 22 = chunk with 5-frame overlap for causal blending?). Actually: chunk of 22 frames where the first 5 are overlap with the previous chunk and 17 are new → snap 5+17k! First chunk yields 22 frames... no wait, first chunk: 5 frames? Hmm: f=5 gives T=2; f=22 gives T=7 (2+5). If decode chunks of latent size 7 produce 22 frames, and consecutive chunks share 2 latent frames (5 pixel frames) of overlap, then chunks advance by 5 latents = 17 pixels, and total = 22 + 17(k-1) = 5 + 17k with k chunks... but f=5 (T=2) is a degenerate single mini-chunk. So: VAE decode window = 7 latent frames → 22 pixel frames, overlap 2 latents / 5 pixels. And the VAE decoder was *trained* on 22-frame windows — hence \"trained 22-frame decoder chunk.\" \n\nNow the DiT: what temporal length does it see? If the DiT also operates on 22-frame (T=7) windows with overlap-conditioning (FL2VA!), then generation of long videos chains windows. But then frame-0 corruption at 107 can't happen from chaining... unless the DiT sees full T. Alternatively the DiT was trained on variable lengths up to some max and the port runs full length; if max trained T is, say, 12? no... Let me think about what max the DiT could have: MiniMax-Video-01 (Hailuo) — the \"Video-01\" model: trained with 3D RoPE? I recall MiniMax-01 LLM used lightning attention; the video model details are less public. FL2VA — searching memory: \"FL2V\" = \"First-Last-frame to Video\"? MiniMax had \"I2V-01\" image-to-video, \"T2V-01\", and \"S2V-01\" subject reference. \"FL2V-","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me think carefully about this problem. The user has a port of MiniMax H3 (FL2VA — frame-to-latent video?) running on AMD eGPU via Metal/MPSGraph. 22 frames clean, 39 frames clean, 107 and 124 corrupt. Corruption is non-progressive, from frame 0, uniform mush at 107, dark noise at 124. Latent temporal depth: t = ((f-5)/17)*5 + 2. So 22→7, 39→12, 56→17, 73→22, 90→27, 107→32, 124→37.\n\nKey constraints: not resources, not divisibility by 22, spatial tiling constant, VAE decodes in temporal chunks (peak memory constant), corruption not progressive, two corruption modes.\n\nLet me think about what's special between latent_t 12 and 32, or between frame 39 and 56.\n\nFirst: h3_video_latent_t(f) = ((f-5)/17)*5 + 2. Interesting. Why 5 and 17? This smells like the FL2VA trick: the VAE is causal temporal with compression 4×, but the first frame compresses to 1 latent frame, and subsequent groups of 4 frames → 1 latent. Standard HunyuanVideo/Wan-style: latent_t = (f-1)/4 + 1. Here it's ((f-5)/17)*5 + 2 — that's a different structure: chunks of 17 frames map to 5 latent frames plus 2 base. Hmm, that suggests the pixel-space chunk of 17 frames maps to 5 latent frames via some 4× temporal compression with padding: (17+3)/4 = 5. And the base 2 comes from the first chunk... Actually maybe the first 5 frames → 2 latent frames? Like first frame → 1 latent, then next 4 → 1 latent. So f=5 → t=2. Then each additional 17 frames adds 5 latents. Hmm, 17 = 1 + 16 = 1 + 4*4? If the pattern repeats: each subsequent chunk is 1 + 4k frames → 1 + k latents. For 5 latents you need 1+16 = 17 frames. That fits a causal temporal VAE with 4× compression where each decode chunk of 5 latent frames yields 17 pixel frames except the first which yields 5 (2 latents → 5 frames: 1 + 4).\n\nOK so structure: DiT latent sequence temporal length T = 2 + 5k. DiT processes the whole latent sequence jointly (temporal attention or 3D RoPE over space+time). Now the trained temporal length: the model was presumably trained on clips of some length. FL2VA... The error message \"generation requires at least one trained 22-frame decoder chunk\" — 22 frames = latent_t 7. Hmm, \"22-frame decoder chunk\" suggests the VAE decode chunk is 22 frames? But our decomposition says 5 + 17k. Hmm, 22 = 5 + 17. So one \"decoder chunk\" is 22 frames? Actually the snap is 5+17k; minimum 22 required by h3.c:861. So 22 = 5 + 17, i.e., latent_t 7 = 2 + 5. Maybe the model was trained with temporal latent length 7? Or the DiT was trained on 22-frame chunks with latent_t = 7?\n\nWait — the key question: is there a trained max sequence length? If the DiT was trained at fixed latent_t = 7 (22 frames) and the port does full-sequence attention with 3D RoPE, then extrapolating to T=32 would degrade. But then 39 frames (T=12) would presumably already be degraded if the max were 7... unless FL2VA uses a chunked/overlap scheme where the DiT itself is applied over windows and the temporal axis beyond some point is handled by... hmm.\n\nFL2VA — I recall MiniMax released \"Hailuo\" video models; H3 maybe \"Hailuo 3\"? FL2VA might stand for \"frame-level latent to video autoencoder\"? Actually, in antirez's h3.c, the design was for a fixed 22-frame model? Upstream antirez/h3.c — I'm not deeply familiar. Let me reason from the given numbers.\n\nObservations to exploit:\n\n1. Corruption onset between T=12 and T=32. Untested: 17, 22, 27.\n\n2. Uniform corruption from frame 0 — this argues the DiT output latents are wrong globally, not a VAE chunk-boundary issue. Because if the VAE temporal chunking were broken, you'd expect some chunks correct and others wrong, or seams every 17 frames. Faint tile seams at 107 though. But frame 0 wrong means either the latents feeding the VAE are globally wrong (DiT output bad), or the very first VAE chunk decode is broken by something that depends on total T (e.g., a buffer sized by T, an RoPE table sized by T, a normalization over T, attention mask sized wrong, etc.).\n\n3. Timing healthy at 107, encode blowup at 124 only. Both corrupt. So corruption mechanism is present at 107 without encode blowup; encode blowup is either independent or a threshold effect of the same root cause at bigger size. But careful: 124 differs from 107 in what? T=37 vs 32. Something crosses a threshold between 32 and 37 — e.g., a buffer exceeding 16 GiB? No — VRAM peaks were equal (2.289 GiB DiT peak). Hmm, the DiT peak was 2.289 GiB in all four runs? \"DiT peak 2.289 GiB, video VAE peak 9.365 GiB\" — it says peaks in all four runs for VAE; presumably DiT peak also constant (attention maybe windowed/chunked?). If DiT peak memory is constant across T, then attention over time must be windowed or the DiT processes temporal chunks separately! That's a big clue: DiT peak constant at 2.289 GiB across T=7..37 implies the DiT does NOT materialize full T×T attention, OR it processes the sequence in temporal windows with cross-window state (FL2VA = maybe \"first-last frame to video\"?). Hmm wait, FL2VA could be \"Frame-Latent 2 Video Autoencoder\"? Or the model processes 22-frame windows and blends?\n\nActually, \"generation requires at least one trained 22-frame decoder chunk\" — maybe the DiT operates on chunks of up to 22 frames (latent 7?), and longer videos are generated by sliding window with overlap, where the model conditions on the last latent frames of the previous chunk (like a FIFO diffusion / history-conditioned rollout). FL2VA might be exactly that: \"Frame-Conditioned Latent Video Autoregression\"? If generation is autoregressive over 22-frame chunks, then corruption uniform from frame 0 can't come from chunk k>0... unless the first chunk itself is generated differently when total length is long (e.g., the sampler allocates per-T buffers, or the scheduler's sigma schedule depends on T, or classifier-free guidance batching splits differently).\n\nHold on — frame 0 of the *video* is wrong, but which DiT chunk produced frame 0? The first chunk. If corruption were in chunk k≥1, frames 0..21 would be clean. So the corruption must affect the first chunk's generation itself, meaning some global parameter that depends on total frame count poisons even the first chunk. Candidates:\n\n- A length-dependent buffer/index bug (stride computed from total T, slicing wrong).\n- RoPE frequencies computed with a table sized by total T but applied per-chunk with wrong offset — but if chunks are processed independently with positions 0..6, a global-T-dependent bug could still shift phases.\n- Attention softmax over a wrong axis when T large (e.g., flattening order changes with padding).\n- The latent buffer is laid out [T, C, H, W] and some kernel assumes T ≤ some constant or uses a strides field that overflows (uint16 ushort indexing!). BF16 stored as ushort — but indexes? If any index or count is stored in ushort/uint16, overflow at 65535 elements. Let's check magnitudes: latent shape maybe [T, 16, 22, 38] for 608×352 → 352/16=22, 608/16=38. T=37 → 37*16*22*38 = 494,  37*16 = 592; 592*22*38 = 494,  592*836 = 494,912. Under 65535. Token count: T × (22×38/4?) If patchify 2×2: 11×19=209 tokens per frame ×37 = 7,733 tokens. Under 65535. Attention matrix 7733² = 59.8M — over 65535 but nobody stores that in ushort. Pixel count: 608×352×3×124 = 79.7M. Byte offsets: activations maybe 2 bytes each → offsets in bytes could exceed 2^31? 494,912 latents ×... hidden dimension say 3072, sequence 7733 → hidden activations 7733×3072 = 23.76M elements, 47.5 MB in BF16 — fine in int32. Attention scores 7733² = 59.8M floats = 239 MB — fits int32 count (59.8M < 2^31). At T=32: tokens 6688, attn 44.7M. Not an overflow boundary between 12 and 32. If attention is computed in blocks, fine.\n\n- The noise schedule: steps=4 fixed; sigmas fixed. Not T-dependent.\n\n- The latents themselves: maybe the corruption is in the video VAE decode, which processes temporal chunks. If the VAE decode is causal (each output chunk depends on previous latent frames via cached conv states), a state-handling bug across chunk boundaries would corrupt later chunks, not frame 0. Unless the chunk loop runs in reverse or the cache is initialized from the *end*. If chunks decoded in an order where the first chunk's decode reads a cache polluted by a later chunk (e.g., cache buffer reused, not reset, and chunks processed 5,4,3,2,1,0 or the cache persists from the previous denoise/VAE encode), then frame 0 could be corrupted by stale state whose size/content depends on T. Hmm, at 22/39 frames, maybe only 1-2 chunks so less opportunity; but T=12 at 39 frames is already 3 chunks (2+5+5). Hmm wait: T=7 → chunks of latent: 2 then 5 → pixel 5 then 17 → 22. T=12 → 2+5+5 → 5+17+17=39. T=32 → 2+5×6 → 5+17×6=107. T=37 → 2+5×7=124. So chunk counts: 22f→2 chunks, 39f→3, 56f→4, 73f→5, 90f→6, 107f→7, 124f→8.\n\nIf the VAE had a chunk-boundary bug, 39 would show it (3 chunks). Unless the bug is a cache that only overflows its buffer after N chunks — e.g., causal conv cache sized for k frames of history, ring buffer overwrite. But frame 0 is wrong at 107, which is the *first* chunk — a causal decoder processing chunk 0 first with a cold cache can't be corrupted by later chunks unless processing order is reversed or cache is pre-populated.\n\nAlternatively the corruption is in the DiT and uniform across all latents — flat brown mush = latents decoded from a wrong but smooth distribution; dark noise = latents with garbage high-frequency energy. Uniform frame-0 corruption is exactly what a global DiT failure looks like: all output latents wrong similarly.\n\nSo the prime suspect region is the DiT forward pass as a function of T. What in a DiT changes qualitatively between T=12 and T=32?\n\n- 3D RoPE: temporal positions 0..T-1. If trained with max temporal position 15 or so (e.g., trained at 65 frames → T=17? or trained at 93 frames?), extrapolation beyond trained length degrades smoothly and globally, worse with distance, affecting all frames jointly through attention. Non-progressive in the sense that frame 0 attends to far frames with OOD positions → all outputs wrong. Severity grows with T: at T=32 mush, at T=37 noise. And clean at 12. That's hypothesis: trained max latent_t between 12 and 32. If H3 was trained on e.g. 5-second 24fps clips = 121 frames → T = 37? Hmm 124 frames at 24 fps ≈ 5.17 s — the table says 5.17 s at 124 frames! And MiniMax Hailuo is known for 6-second clips at 24fps = 129-145 frames? Hailuo 02 generates 6s at 768p or 10s? Hmm. The table's \"seconds\" column: 22→0.92, 39→1.63, 107→4.46, 124→5.17 — so 24 fps. Hailuo/MiniMax clips: 6 s at 24 fps = 144 frames → snap 5+17k → 141 → T = ((141-5)/17)*5+2 = 42. Or the model trained at 129 frames → snap 124? 129 isn't 5+17k. Trained length might be 124 frames exactly = T=37? But 124 is corrupt, worse than 107. So trained max T is probably below 32? But 39 (T=12) clean and 22 (T=7) clean.\n\nWait — but there's the FL2VA name again. Maybe the architecture: Frame-Latent-to-Video-Autoregressive? If the model natively generates 22 frames per chunk and chains, then per-chunk positions are always 0..6 and RoPE never extrapolates. Then long-video corruption from RoPE doesn't apply per chunk... but then why would corruption appear at all at 107? Unless the chaining state (conditioning on previous chunk's last frames) degrades: error accumulation across autoregressive chunks. But that's progressive — frame 0 would be clean. Contradiction with observation 5 (frame 0 wrong at 107). Unless... the user measured YAVG of the *first frames* — frame 0 is in chunk 0. Wrong from frame 0 means chunk 0 latents are wrong. In an autoregressive scheme chunk 0 doesn't depend on chain state. So a global bug.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the DiT does full-sequence attention over all T latent frames (joint 3D attention, like CogVideoX/HunyuanVideo). DiT peak constant 2.289 GiB could still hold if attention is flash-style tiled (memory O(N) not O(N²)) and weights dominate. Weights streamed: 144.272 GiB moved over the run, at 1.486 GiB/s. If weights are streamed from host RAM (Thunderbolt 3 20 Gb/s ≈ 2.5 GB/s theoretical, ~1.5-2 achievable), 16 GiB VRAM can't hold the model (MiniMax H3 maybe ~14B params in BF16 ≈ 28 GB — must stream). So peak DiT 2.289 GiB = streaming buffers + activations, and activations grow with T but maybe capped small vs weights. Constant peak could be because activation peak is dominated by per-layer buffers reused across layers; sequence length 6688 vs 2732 tokens changes activation sizes linearly: 7733×3072×2 bytes = 47.5 MB vs 2732×3072×2=16.8 MB — small vs 2.289 GiB. So constant peak doesn't rule out full attention. OK.\n\nSo full-joint-attention DiT with 3D RoPE, trained at some max T. Clean at 12, corrupt at 32. Typical training: Hailuo-01 trained 128 frames? If trained temporal latent length is 16 (i.e., 61 frames? doesn't align to 5+17k)... Hmm. Alternatively trained with T up to 16 via rope and NTK/positional extrapolation not applied in the port. Between 12 and 32 lies 17 — suspicious because 17 is the pixel chunk and 5 the latent chunk. Could the trained max be T=17? Hmm